As an AI assistant, I must clarify that it is not possible to locate the x-intercept of an equation on an HTML page using C++ code. HTML is a markup language used for creating web pages, while C++ is a programming language used for developing software applications. However, I can provide you with an example of C++ code that can be used to find the x-intercept of the equation y = 2x + 4 and ensure that the slope of the tangent line at the intercept is positive.
Here's the code:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
    double x_intercept = -2; // initial guess for x-intercept
    double slope = 2; // slope of the line
    double y_intercept = 4; // y-intercept of the line
    double epsilon = 1e-6; // tolerance for convergence
    double error = 1; // initial error
    while (error > epsilon) {
        double y = slope * x_intercept + y_intercept; // calculate y-value at x-intercept
        double slope_at_x = slope; // slope of the tangent line at x-intercept
        error = abs(y); // calculate error
        if (slope_at_x < 0) {
            x_intercept -= error / slope_at_x; // adjust x-intercept
        } else {
            x_intercept += error / slope_at_x; // adjust x-intercept
        }
    }
    cout << "x-intercept: " << x_intercept << endl;
    cout << "slope of tangent line: " << slope << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we use the Newton-Raphson method to find the x-intercept of the line y = 2x + 4. We start with an initial guess for the x-intercept (-2) and calculate the y-value at that point. We then calculate the slope of the tangent line at that point (which is just the slope of the line itself, since the line is a straight line). If the slope is negative, we adjust the x-intercept to the left; if it's positive, we adjust it to the right. We repeat this process until the error (which is just the absolute value of the y-value at the current x-intercept) is less than a certain tolerance (epsilon).
Once we have found the x-intercept, we output it to the console, along with the slope of the tangent line at that point. Note that since the slope of the line is positive, the slope of the tangent line at the x-intercept will also be positive.

