How can an HTML page visually represent the hydrologic cycle?
In HTML, the hydrologic cycle can be illustrated through the use of a diagram or animation. One way to do this is to create an SVG image that displays the various components of the cycle, such as evaporation, precipitation, and runoff. Another option is to use HTML5 canvas to create an interactive animation that demonstrates the process. 
For example, here is some sample HTML code for an SVG image of the hydrologic cycle:
<svg width="400" height="400">
  <circle cx="200" cy="200" r="150" fill="blue" />
  <circle cx="200" cy="200" r="125" fill="white" />
  <path d="M200 75 C275 75, 325 125, 325 200" stroke="white" stroke-width="5" fill="none" />
  <path d="M200 325 C125 325, 75 275, 75 200" stroke="white" stroke-width="5" fill="none" />
  <path d="M200 75 C125 75, 75 125, 75 200" stroke="white" stroke-width="5" fill="none" />
  <path d="M200 325 C275 325, 325 275, 325 200" stroke="white" stroke-width="5" fill="none" />
  <rect x="175" y="25" width="50" height="25" fill="white" />
  <text x="200" y="40" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">Evaporation</text>
  <rect x="175" y="350" width="50" height="25" fill="white" />
  <text x="200" y="365" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">Precipitation</text>
  <rect x="0" y="175" width="25" height="50" fill="white" />
  <text x="12" y="200" font-size="12" text-anchor="start">Runoff</text>
</svg>
This code creates a blue circle to represent water, and four white circles and curved lines to show the flow of water through the cycle. Text labels are added to indicate the different stages of the cycle.
Alternatively, here is an example of how HTML5 canvas could be used to create an animated hydrologic cycle:
<canvas id="hydrocycle" width="400" height="400"></canvas>
<script>
  var canvas = document.getElementById("hydrocycle");
  var ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
  var waterLevel = 200;
  var evapRate = 0.1;
  var precipRate = 0.2;
  var runoffRate = 0.05;
  function draw() {
    ctx.fillStyle = "blue";
    ctx.fillRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
    ctx.fillStyle = "white";
    ctx.fillRect(0, waterLevel, canvas.width, canvas.height - waterLevel);
    ctx.fillStyle = "green";
    ctx.fillRect(150, 150, 100, 50);
    ctx.fillStyle = "black";
    ctx.font = "12px Arial";
    ctx.fillText("Evaporation", 200, 100);
    ctx.fillText("Precipitation", 200, 300);
    ctx.fillText("Runoff", 90, 180);
    waterLevel -= evapRate;
    waterLevel += precipRate;
    waterLevel -= runoffRate;
    requestAnimationFrame(draw);
  }
  draw();
</script>
This code creates a canvas element and uses JavaScript to draw and animate the hydrologic cycle. The blue background represents water, and rectangles are used to show the different stages of the cycle. The evaporation, precipitation, and runoff rates are adjusted every frame to simulate the movement of water.

