To find the number of words in a string that contain all five vowels in alphabetical order, you can use the following Java code:
```java
public static int countWordsWithAllVowels(String input) {
    int count = 0;
    Set<String> seenWords = new HashSet<>();
    String[] words = input.split("\\s+");
    for (String word : words) {
        if (seenWords.contains(word)) {
            continue;
        }
        if (word.matches(".*a.*e.*i.*o.*u.*")) {
            seenWords.add(word);
            count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}
```
This code uses a regular expression to check if a word contains all five vowels in alphabetical order. It also uses a `Set` to keep track of the words that have already been counted, so that the same word is not counted multiple times.
To use this code, you can call the `countWordsWithAllVowels` method with a string as the input:
```java
String input = "The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog. The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.";
