To automatically generate citations for various sources in a research paper using PHP code, you can create a function that accepts the source type, author name, title, publication date, publisher, and other relevant information as input parameters. Based on the source type, the function can then apply the appropriate citation format and return the formatted citation string.
Here's an example code that showcases the implementation of this citation generation method:
```php
function generateCitation($sourceType, $author, $title, $publicationDate, $publisher, $url = null) {
  // Initialize the citation string
  $citation = '';
  // Apply the appropriate citation format based on the source type
  switch ($sourceType) {
    case 'HTML page':
      $citation = $author . '. "' . $title . '." Web. ' . $publicationDate . ' <' . $url . '>.';
      break;
    case 'Journal article':
      $citation = $author . '. "' . $title . '." ' . $publisher . ', ' . $publicationDate . '.';
      break;
    case 'Book':
      $citation = $author . '. ' . $title . '. ' . $publisher . ', ' . $publicationDate . '.';
      break;
    default:
      $citation = 'Invalid source type.';
  }
  // Return the formatted citation string
  return $citation;
}
// Example usage
$htmlCitation = generateCitation('HTML page', 'John Doe', 'Lorem Ipsum', '2021', 'Lorem Ipsum Inc.', 'https://www.example.com');
$journalCitation = generateCitation('Journal article', 'Jane Smith', 'Dolor Sit Amet', '2020', 'Journal of Lorem Ipsum');
$bookCitation = generateCitation('Book', 'James Johnson', 'Consectetur Adipiscing Elit', '2019', 'Lorem Ipsum Press');
echo $htmlCitation . '<br>';
echo $journalCitation . '<br>';
echo $bookCitation . '<br>';
```
In this example, the `generateCitation()` function takes in the source type, author name, title, publication date, publisher, and URL (for HTML pages) as input parameters. It then uses a switch statement to apply the appropriate citation format based on the source type. Finally, the function returns the formatted citation string.
The example usage section showcases how to call the `generateCitation()` function with different input parameters to generate citations for an HTML page, a journal article, and a book. The output of the example code will be:
```
John Doe. "Lorem Ipsum." Web. 2021 <https://www.example.com>.
Jane Smith. "Dolor Sit Amet." Journal of Lorem Ipsum, 2020.
James Johnson. Consectetur Adipiscing Elit. Lorem Ipsum Press, 2019.
```
Note that this is just a basic example and the citation formats may vary depending on the citation style guide being used (e.g. MLA, APA, Chicago). You may need to modify the `generateCitation()` function to adhere to the specific citation style guide requirements.

